(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-phases-of-sleep-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Phases of Sleep Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 30-year-old resident falls asleep after a 30-hour shift without any sleep. What is expected of the rapid eye movement episodes on her recording tonight?","choices":["More frequent than usual","Short or even absent","Longer than on rested nights","The same as on rested nights"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When a person is extremely sleepy, each rapid eye movement episode is short or even absent; as the person becomes more rested, the episodes lengthen. After 30 hours awake she is extremely sleepy, so tonight her rapid eye movement episodes should be brief or missing. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 6"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman believes that sleep matters for the brain and nothing else. Which statement about the physiological effects of sleep is accurate?","choices":["Its effects are purely psychological","It affects brain and body systems","It affects the brain alone","It affects the muscles alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sleep has two major types of physiological effects: effects on the nervous system and effects on the other functional systems of the body. Its functions span both, from neural maturation and cognition to conservation of metabolic energy, so its importance is not limited to the brain. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 17, 12, 10"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman's recording during a period of rapid eye movements shows irregular, high-frequency waves, and the technician labels it desynchronized sleep. What does that label describe?","choices":["Rhythmic waves of quiet rest","Fatigue of the activating system","Neurons firing without synchrony","Neurons firing in tight unison"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep is also called desynchronized sleep because there is a lack of synchrony in neuron firing when there is brain activity. The tracing is irregular and high frequency, much like an awake, active person's, which fits her period of rapid eye movements. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 29, 22, 19"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman lies in bed at lights-out with her eyes closed. Her electroencephalogram shows rhythmical waves at 8 to 13 cycles per second, strongest over the occipital region. Which state does this tracing represent?","choices":["Alert wakefulness","Stage N1 sleep","Rapid eye movement sleep","Quiet wakefulness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rhythmical waves at 8 to 13 cycles per second, strongest over the occipital region, are alpha waves, the mark of quiet wakefulness: an awake person with the eyes closed, relaxed, with a decreased level of alertness. She has not yet fallen asleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 27, 28, 29, 9"},{"q":"A 23-year-old healthy man has a routine electroencephalogram while awake and resting quietly. How common is an alpha rhythm in healthy adults in this state?","choices":["Found in almost all of them","Found in about half of them","Found in a small minority","Found mainly in children"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alpha waves are found in almost all healthy adults when they are awake and in a quiet, resting state, occurring most intensely over the occipital region and also from the parietal and frontal regions. An alpha rhythm in this healthy, resting 23-year-old is the expected finding. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 25"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man lies relaxed with his eyes closed while his electroencephalogram shows a steady alpha rhythm. The study next calls for a recording of his beta waves. What should he be asked to do?","choices":["Focus on a specific mental task","Keep resting with his eyes closed","Drift off into light sleep","Think about a past disappointment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When an awake person's attention is directed to a specific type of mental activity, alpha waves stop and are replaced by beta waves, faster than 14 cycles per second, asynchronous and low voltage, recorded mainly from the frontal and parietal regions. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 23, 22, 25, 27"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman in a sleep study is in rapid eye movement sleep, with atonia of her voluntary muscles. Which muscles keep working during this atonia?","choices":["Diaphragm but not eye muscles","Eye muscles but not diaphragm","Eye muscles and diaphragm","Arm and leg muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In rapid eye movement sleep the voluntary muscles are atonic, except the eye muscles and the diaphragm, because alpha motor neurons are inhibited. The spared eye muscles produce the irregular, sharply peaked rapid eye movements, and the spared diaphragm keeps her breathing going, though irregularly. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 10"},{"q":"A 19-year-old student learning anatomy asks which function of sleep helps her retain what she studies. Which function is it?","choices":["Facilitating learning or memory","Targeted erasure of synapses","Conserving metabolic energy","Clearing metabolic waste"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Facilitation of learning or memory is one of the functions of sleep, alongside cognition, and it is the function tied to retaining what she studies. Restricting sleep over a few days lowers cognitive performance, which works against her. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 17, 16"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has had several short nights, and his first recovery night showed short rapid eye movement episodes. He asks what will happen to those episodes as he catches up on sleep. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["They will get shorter","They will stay the same","They will stop occurring","They will get longer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement episode length follows sleepiness: when a person is extremely sleepy each episode is short or even absent, and as the person becomes more rested (less sleepy), the duration of the episodes increases. Catching up on sleep should lengthen his episodes. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman in a sleep study has an electroencephalogram resembling wakefulness with irregular, sharply peaked eye movements. What is expected of her limb muscle tone during this period?","choices":["Markedly depressed","Higher than when awake","The same as when awake","Kept in limbs, lost in eyes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her waking-like tracing and irregular, sharply peaked eye movements place her in rapid eye movement sleep, in which muscle tone is very depressed: the voluntary muscles show atonia, sparing the eye muscles and diaphragm, from inhibition of spinal muscle control areas and alpha motor neurons. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 28"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with a degenerative brain disorder has an electroencephalogram while awake and calm. It shows waves at 4 to 7 cycles per second. Which explanation best fits these waves?","choices":["Her calm, relaxed state","Very deep sleep","Her brain disorder","Her emotional stress"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Waves at 4 to 7 cycles per second are theta waves, which occur in many brain disorders, especially degenerative brain states. In a calm, awake adult with a known degenerative brain disorder, that disorder best explains them. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 25, 22, 26"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman wakes on her own at the end of a full night's sleep, recalling a vivid dream. From which sleep stage did she probably wake?","choices":["Rapid eye movement sleep","Non-rapid eye movement stage N3","Non-rapid eye movement stage N2","Non-rapid eye movement stage N1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep is associated with vivid dreaming, and people can awaken spontaneously in the morning during rapid eye movement sleep; in a typical night the last rapid eye movement period leads into waking. Her spontaneous morning waking and vivid dream both point here. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 13, 14, 7, 28"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man is awake and alert, working through mental arithmetic during an electroencephalogram. Over his frontal and parietal regions the tracing shows asynchronous, low-voltage waves at 25 cycles per second. Which waveform is this?","choices":["Delta waves","Beta waves","Alpha waves","Theta waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta waves occur at frequencies greater than 14 cycles per second (as high as 80), are asynchronous and low voltage, and are recorded mainly from the parietal and frontal regions while a person is awake, alert and actively processing information, as he is during mental arithmetic. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 23, 24, 22, 25, 26"},{"q":"A 24-year-old competitive cyclist has cut her sleep to 5 hours a night for the 4 nights before a race. Which effect on her race performance is expected?","choices":["No change after so few nights","Faster times from a lower heart rate","Lower physical performance","Higher endurance from training more"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sleep restriction over a few days can decrease physical as well as cognitive performance, along with productivity and health. Four nights of 5 hours before her race is restriction over a few days, so her physical performance is expected to fall. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 14"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has rhythmical alpha waves on his electroencephalogram while resting with his eyes closed. When he opens his eyes, the tracing flattens into faster, low-voltage activity. Which waveform has replaced the alpha waves?","choices":["Theta waves","Delta waves","Sawtooth waves","Beta waves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With the eyes closed and relaxed, an awake adult shows alpha waves; with the eyes open the person is alert and active, which brings beta waves, fast and low voltage. On the tracing, alpha flattens into this faster, low-voltage activity when the eyes open and returns when they close. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 28, 23, 25, 26"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman says she no longer sleeps as deeply as she did in her twenties. Which sleep stage can she be told decreases with age?","choices":["Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N2","Stage N1","Stage N3"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage N3, the deep, restful, deepest non-rapid eye movement stage with delta waves, decreases with age. Her sense that her sleep is less deep than it was in her twenties fits a smaller share of stage N3 sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 6"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has been awake and active for many hours, and the feedback loop between her reticular nuclei and her cerebral cortex is fading. Which centers are proposed to take over at this point?","choices":["Reticular activating nuclei","Cerebral cortex","Spinal muscle control areas","Sleep-promoting centers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After the brain has remained active for many hours, neurons in the activating system fatigue, the positive feedback cycle between the mesencephalic reticular nuclei and the cerebral cortex fades, and the sleep-promoting centers of the sleep centers take over, bringing the transition from wakefulness to sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 19, 18, 10"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman asks what sleep does about the by-products her brain makes while she is awake and thinking. Which function answers her?","choices":["Targeted erasure of synapses","Raising brain metabolism","Clearing metabolic waste","Conserving metabolic energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One function of sleep is clearance of metabolic waste products generated by neural activity in the awake brain. Her question about the by-products of her brain's waking activity is answered by that clearance function. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 17, 10"},{"q":"A 2-month-old boy's electroencephalogram shows slow waves below 3.5 cycles per second. Compared with most other types of brain waves, how large is their voltage expected to be?","choices":["Much smaller","About the same","2 to 4 times greater","20 to 40 times greater"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Delta waves, which include all waves below 3.5 cycles per second and occur in infancy, often have voltages 2 to 4 times greater than most other types of brain waves. On a tracing they stand out as tall, slow waves, in contrast to the low-voltage beta waves of an alert adult. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 21, 23"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman wakes in the morning and stays awake through the day without effort. According to the proposed sleep-wake theory, what keeps her wakefulness going once it begins?","choices":["Rising parasympathetic tone","Reticular-cortex positive feedback","Continuous activation of sleep centers","Fatigue of activating-system neurons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Once wakefulness begins, it can sustain itself naturally because of a positive feedback mechanism: the reticular activating nuclei excite the cerebral cortex and peripheral nervous system, which send positive feedback signals back to the same nuclei and keep them active. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 19, 14"},{"q":"A 20-year-old college student is slumped over a library desk with his eyes closed. A friend says his name quietly and he does not respond, but when the friend shakes his arm he wakes and starts talking. Which finding shows he was asleep rather than in a coma?","choices":["He ignored a quiet voice","His eyes were closed","He lay slumped and motionless","He woke when his arm was shaken"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sleep is unconsciousness from which a person can be aroused by sensory or other stimuli; coma is unconsciousness from which a person cannot be aroused. Waking when his arm was shaken shows he was arousable, so he was asleep, even though a quieter stimulus had failed first. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5"},{"q":"A 24-year-old student has been awake and studying for 20 hours and finally dozes off at his desk. Which proposed mechanism explains his transition from wakefulness to sleep?","choices":["Increased parasympathetic tone","Fatigue of activating-system neurons","Stronger reticular-cortex positive feedback","Release of reticular nuclei from inhibition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After the brain has remained active for many hours, neurons in the activating system become fatigued, the positive feedback cycle between the mesencephalic reticular nuclei and the cerebral cortex fades, and the sleep-promoting centers take over. Twenty hours of studying fits that fatigue. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 19, 18, 14"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man asks whether scientists fully understand why sleep is essential. Which reply is accurate?","choices":["No; it seems to have no real function","No; its functions are still under study","Yes; it exists to save energy","Yes; it mainly rests the muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sleep has important physiological functions, but researchers have limited understanding of why it is an essential part of life; its specific physiological functions remain a mystery and continue to be researched. Its functions include neural maturation, learning or memory, cognition, waste clearance and energy conservation. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman lies relaxed with her eyes closed at the start of a sleep study, and her electroencephalogram shows alpha waves. What happens to these alpha waves once she reaches deep sleep?","choices":["They speed up into beta","They persist unchanged","They disappear","They grow larger"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alpha waves belong to quiet, relaxed wakefulness, such as resting with the eyes closed, and they disappear during deep sleep. As she sinks into deep non-rapid eye movement sleep the tracing slows progressively, until delta waves below 3.5 cycles per second take over in stage N3. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 26, 27, 24, 28"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman in a sleep laboratory has passed beyond drowsy, light sleep. She is now less responsive, her heart rate and temperature are falling, and her electroencephalogram shows theta waves. Which stage is she in?","choices":["Stage N1","Stage N3","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage N2 is less responsive, deeper sleep in which heart rate and temperature decrease, and its electroencephalogram shows theta waves. She has moved past the drowsy, light sleep of stage N1 but does not show the delta waves of stage N3. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 14"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man in a sleep laboratory has been in deep, restful non-rapid eye movement sleep for 30 minutes. Which change in his vital signs is expected?","choices":["Irregular heart rate and breathing","Higher blood pressure and heart rate","Unchanged from wakefulness","Lower blood pressure and heart rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-rapid eye movement sleep brings increased parasympathetic tone with low blood pressure, slow heart rate and decreased respiratory rate. Blood pressure, heart rate, respiratory rate and metabolic rate fall by about 10 to 30%, along with decreased peripheral vascular tone. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 14, 10"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man is awake and answering questions during an electroencephalogram. The tracing shows high-voltage waves below 3.5 cycles per second. Which explanation best fits this finding?","choices":["Quiet, relaxed wakefulness","Serious organic brain disease","Very deep sleep","Emotional stress"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High-voltage waves below 3.5 cycles per second are delta waves, which occur in very deep sleep, in infancy and in persons with serious organic brain disease. He is an awake adult answering questions, which rules out deep sleep and infancy, so serious organic brain disease is the setting that fits. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 25, 22"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man's blood pressure, heart rate, breathing rate and metabolic rate are tracked as he passes into deep, restful non-rapid eye movement sleep. By about how much are these values expected to fall?","choices":["1 to 5 percent","10 to 30 percent","40 to 60 percent","70 to 90 percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Deep, restful non-rapid eye movement sleep is associated with decreased peripheral vascular tone and other vegetative bodily functions: blood pressure, heart rate, respiratory rate and metabolic rate fall by about 10 to 30%, alongside increased parasympathetic tone. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 14, 10"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has read that the dreaming stage is the most restful part of the night. Which statement corrects him?","choices":["Dreaming sleep is not restful or deep","Dreaming sleep is restful but light","The brain rests most while dreaming","Restfulness is equal across stages"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep, the stage associated with vivid dreaming, is not the most restful type of sleep: it is not restful or deep, and the brain is very active. The deep, restful sleep is non-rapid eye movement sleep, and stage N3 is its deepest, most restful stage. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 9, 10, 28"},{"q":"A 16-year-old athlete asks during which stage of sleep body tissue growth and repair take place. Which stage should he be told?","choices":["Stage N3","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N2","Stage N1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage N3, the deep, restful, deepest non-rapid eye movement stage, is when body and tissue growth and repair occur. Its electroencephalogram shows delta waves, and it is the stage that decreases with age. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 6, 10"}]};
